helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Drop Caps				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	bm_id7635731				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>first letters;starting paragraphs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>capital letters;starting paragraphs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>drop caps;inserting\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150252				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/swriter/01/05030400.xhp\" name=\"Drop Caps\"\>Drop Caps\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3154763				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DROPCAPS\"\>Formats the first letter of a paragraph with a large capital letter, that can span several lines. You can also format the first word of the paragraph with large type.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151388				0	bs	Settings				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147295				0	bs	Show Drop Caps				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3150536				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_CHECKBOX_TP_DROPCAPS_CB_SWITCH\"\>Applies the drop cap settings to the selected paragraph.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155626				0	bs	Whole word				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3154554				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_CHECKBOX_TP_DROPCAPS_CB_WORD\"\>Displays the first letter of the first word in the paragraph as a drop cap, and the remaining letters of the word as large type.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154505				0	bs	Number of characters				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3149881	8			0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_NUMERICFIELD_TP_DROPCAPS_FLD_DROPCAPS\"\>Enter the number of characters to convert to drop caps. \</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150932				0	bs	Lines				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3148391				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_NUMERICFIELD_TP_DROPCAPS_FLD_LINES\"\>Enter the number of lines that you want the drop cap to extend downward from the first line of the paragraph.\</ahelp\> The selection is limited to 2-9 lines.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149030				0	bs	Distance from text				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3153926				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_METRICFIELD_TP_DROPCAPS_FLD_DISTANCE\"\>Enter the amount of space to leave between the drop caps and the rest of the text in the paragraph.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153723				0	bs	Contents				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154638				0	bs	Text				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3147569				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_EDIT_TP_DROPCAPS_EDT_TEXT\"\>Enter the text that you want to display as drop caps instead of the first letters of the paragraph.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150763				0	bs	Character Style				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3151181				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_LISTBOX_TP_DROPCAPS_BOX_TEMPLATE\"\>Select the formatting style that you want to apply to the drop caps.\</ahelp\> To use the formatting style of the current paragraph, select [None].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\selection_mode.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Selection Mode				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Defining Conditions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145828				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>logical expressions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulating conditions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>conditions; in fields and sections\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>fields;defining conditions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sections;defining conditions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables; in conditions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>display of fields;conditions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>user data;in conditions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>databases in conditions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>hiding; database fields\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145242				0	bs	Conditions are logical expressions that you can use to control the display of \<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04090000.xhp\"\>fields\</link\> and \<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04020000.xhp\"\>sections\</link\> in your document. Although the following examples apply to fields, they also apply to sections.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147171				0	bs	You can define conditions for the following field types:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151185				0	bs	Conditional text: displays text A if the condition is true, or text B if the condition is false.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149289				0	bs	Hidden text: hides the contents of the field if the condition is true.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145412				0	bs	Hidden paragraph: hides the paragraph if the condition is true.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147515				0	bs	Any record and next record: controls the access to database records.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149802	18			0	bs	The simplest way to define a condition is to type the logical expression directly in a \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box using the following values:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153677				0	bs	True				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152960				0	bs	The condition is always met. You can also enter any value not equal to 0 as the conditional text.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155900				0	bs	False				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154191				0	bs	The condition is not met. You can also enter the value 0.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147090	23			0	bs	If you leave the \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box empty, the condition is interpreted as not being met.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148980				0	bs	When you define a condition, use the same \<link href=\"text/swriter/02/14020000.xhp\"\>elements\</link\> for defining a formula, namely comparative operators, mathematical and statistical functions, number formats, variables and constants.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153638				0	bs	You can use the following types of variables when you define a condition:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155135				0	bs	Predefined \<link href=\"text/swriter/02/14020000.xhp\"\>$[officename] variables\</link\> that use statistics on document properties				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156273				0	bs	Custom variables, that are a created with the "Set variable" field				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149174				0	bs	Variables based on user data				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145781				0	bs	Variables based on the contents of database fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155916				0	bs	You cannot use internal variables, such as page and chapter numbers, in condition expression.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151375				0	bs	Conditions and Variables				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150122				0	bs	The following examples use a variable called "x":				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147417				0	bs	x == 1 or x EQ 1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147500				0	bs	The condition is true if "x" is equal to 1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145259				0	bs	x != 1 or x NEQ 1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150551				0	bs	The condition is true if "x" does not equal 1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147749				0	bs	sinx == 0				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146345				0	bs	The condition is true if "x" is a multiple of pi.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149846				0	bs	To use comparative operators with strings, the operands must be bounded by double quotation marks:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151078				0	bs	x == "ABC" or x EQ "ABC"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150162				0	bs	Checks if variable "x" contains (true) the "ABC" string, or not (false).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153301				0	bs	x == "" or x EQ ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156120				0	bs	or				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156133				0	bs	!x or NOT x				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156112				0	bs	Checks if the variable "x" contains an empty string.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150097				0	bs	The "equal" comparative operator must be represented by two equal signs (==) in a condition. For example, if you define a variable "x" with the value of 1, you can enter the condition as x==1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148791				0	bs	User Data				2002-02-02 02:02:02
