helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150147				0	bs	For example, to hide a paragraph, text, or a section from a user with a specific initial, such as "LM", enter the condition: user_initials=="LM".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154115				0	bs	Conditions and Database Fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154128				0	bs	You can define conditions for accessing databases, or database fields. For example, you can check the contents of a database field from a condition, or use database fields in logical expressions. The following table lists a few more examples of using databases in conditions:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156066				0	bs	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156088				0	bs	Meaning				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155948				0	bs	Database.Table.Company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148673				0	bs	Database.Table.Company NEQ ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148687				0	bs	Database.Table.Company != ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155373				0	bs	The condition is true if the COMPANY field is not empty. (In the first example, no operator is required.)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149977				0	bs	!Database.Table.Company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149991				0	bs	NOT Database.Table.Company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150004				0	bs	Database.Table.Company EQ ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146911				0	bs	Database.Table.Company ==""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148733				0	bs	Returns TRUE if the COMPANY field is empty. (Exclamation sign represents a logical NOT.)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148762				0	bs	Database.Table.Company !="Sun Microsystems"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153016				0	bs	Database.Table.Company NEQ "Sun Microsystems"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153040				0	bs	Returns TRUE if the current entry in the COMPANY field is "Sun Microsystems".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154605				0	bs	Database.Table.Firstname AND Database.Table.Name				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153059				0	bs	Returns TRUE if the record contains the first and the last name.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159247				0	bs	Note the difference between the boolean NOT "!" and the comparative operator not equal "!=" (NEQ).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153876				0	bs	When you refer to a database field in a condition, use the form Databasename.Tablename.Fieldname. If one of the names contains a character that is an operator, such as a minus sign (-), enclose the name in square brackets, for example, Databasename.[Table-name].Fieldname. Never use spaces inside field names.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153891				0	bs	Example: Hiding an Empty Database Field				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150051				0	bs	You may want to create a condition that hides an empty field, for example, if the COMPANY field is empty for some of the data records.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150067				0	bs	Select the \<emph\>Hidden Paragraph\</emph\> box, and type the following condition: Addressbook.Addresses.Company EQ ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147110				0	bs	or type the following				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147123				0	bs	NOT Addressbook.Addresses.Company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147136				0	bs	If the COMPANY database field is empty, the condition is true and the paragraph is hidden.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145218				0	bs	Examples of Conditions in Fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145231				0	bs	The following examples use the Conditional text field, although they can be applied to any fields that can be linked to a condition. The syntax used for conditions is also used for the Hidden text, Hidden paragraph, Any record or Next record fields.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150311				0	bs	To display conditional text based on the number of pages:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150333				0	bs	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Fields - Other\</emph\>, and then click the \<emph\>Functions\</emph\> tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147471				0	bs	In the \<emph\>Type\</emph\> list, click "Conditional text".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154294	63			0	bs	In the \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box, type "page == 1".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	bs	In the \<emph\>Then\</emph\> box, type "There is only one page".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150640	65			0	bs	In the \<emph\>Or \</emph\>box, type "There are several pages".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153086				0	bs	Click \<emph\>Insert\</emph\>, and then click \<emph\>Close\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155814				0	bs	To display conditional text based on a user-defined Variable				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155836				0	bs	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Fields - Other\</emph\>, and then click the \<emph\>Variables\</emph\> tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155109	68			0	bs	In the \<emph\>Type \</emph\>list, click "Set Variable".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147008	139			0	bs	In the\<emph\> Name\</emph\> box, type "Profit".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147032	140			0	bs	In the\<emph\> Value\</emph\> box, type "5000".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152974				0	bs	Click \<emph\>Insert\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152998				0	bs	Click the \<emph\>Functions\</emph\> tab, and click "Conditional text" in the \<emph\>Type\</emph\> list.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150952				0	bs	In the \<emph\>Condition\</emph\> box, type "Profit < 5000".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156291				0	bs	In the \<emph\>Then\</emph\> box, type "Target is not met".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156317	72			0	bs	In the \<emph\>Or \</emph\>box, type "Target is met".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154366				0	bs	Click \<emph\>Insert\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154389				0	bs	To edit the contents of the "Profit" variable, double-click the variable field.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155573				0	bs	To display conditional text based on the contents of a database field:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155587				0	bs	The first part of this example inserts a space between the "First Name" and "Last Name" fields in a document, and the second part inserts text based on the contents of a field name. This example requires that an address data source to be registered with $[officename].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150523				0	bs	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Fields - Other\</emph\>, and then click the \<emph\>Database\</emph\> tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148811	75			0	bs	In the \<emph\>Type \</emph\>list, click "Mail merge fields".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148841	76			0	bs	In the\<emph\> Database selection\</emph\> box, double-click an address book, click "First Name", and then click\<emph\> Insert\</emph\>. Repeat for "Last Name".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147549	78			0	bs	In the document, place the cursor between the two fields, press Space, and then return to the \<emph\>Fields \</emph\>dialog:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150416				0	bs	Click the \<emph\>Functions\</emph\> tab, and then click "Conditional text" in the \<emph\>Type\</emph\> list.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153589	146			0	bs	In the \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box, type: "Addressbook.addresses.firstname".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153615	80			0	bs	In the \<emph\>Then \</emph\>box, type a space and leave the \<emph\>Or \</emph\>box blank.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153562				0	bs	You can now use a condition to insert text based on the contents of the First Name field.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150574	147			0	bs	In the \<emph\>Fields \</emph\>dialog, click the \<emph\>Functions \</emph\>tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150605	85			0	bs	In the \<emph\>Type \</emph\>box, click "Conditional text".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151277	86			0	bs	In the \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box, type: Addressbook.addresses.firstname == "Michael"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151303	87			0	bs	In the \<emph\>Then \</emph\>box, type "Dear".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149138				0	bs	In the \<emph\>Or \</emph\>box, type "Hello".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149163				0	bs	Click \<emph\>Insert\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\06110000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Calculate				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\06110000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154505				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/swriter/01/06110000.xhp\" name=\"Calculate\"\>Calculate\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\06110000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150021				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\".uno:CalculateSel\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Calculates the selected formula and copies the result to the clipboard.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\06190000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Update All				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\06190000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145824				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/swriter/01/06190000.xhp\" name=\"Update All\"\>Update All\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\06190000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153004				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\".uno:UpdateAll\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Updates all links, fields, indexes, tables of contents, and page formatting in the current document.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Navigation				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147745				0	bs	Navigation				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149844				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SCRL_NAVI\"\>If you click this icon in the Navigator or in the lower right of the document window, a toolbar will appear which enables you to choose among the existing targets within a document.\</ahelp\> You can then use the up and down arrow icons to position the text cursor in the document on the previous or next target.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153293				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SCRL_PAGEUP\"\>Click the up button to scroll to the previous page or object.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156098				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SCRL_PAGEDOWN\"\>Click the down button to scroll to the next page or object.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155076				0	bs	By default, as long as you have not selected any other entry, the arrow buttons jump to the previous or the next page in the document. The arrow buttons are black if you are browsing through pages and blue if you jump to other objects.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154330				0	bs	The entries largely correspond to those in the \<link href=\"text/swriter/01/02110000.xhp\" name=\"Navigator\"\>Navigator\</link\> selection box. You can also select other jump destinations. An example are the reminders, which you can set with the \<emph\>Set Reminder\</emph\> icon in the Navigator. You can select an object from among the following options on the \<emph\>Navigation\</emph\> toolbar: table, text frame, graphics, OLE object, page, headings, reminder, drawing object, control field, section, bookmark, selection, footnote, note, index entry, table formula, wrong table formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148783				0	bs	For table formulas, you can either jump to all table formulas located within your document or only to the incorrect ones. For incorrect formulas, you jump only to formulas that have resulted in errors. The program skips over formulas with resulting errors (those that reference incorrect formulas).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150031				0	bs	Working With the Navigation Toolbar				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150045				0	bs	Open the \<emph\>Navigation\</emph\> toolbar by clicking on its icon located in the vertical scrollbar. You can break the toolbar away from its place by dragging and arrange it on the screen.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153141				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_NAVI_VS\"\>Click the icon for the type of objects you want to browse through. Then click one of the "Previous Object" or "Next Object" arrow buttons. The names of these buttons indicate the type of object you have selected. The text cursor is placed on whichever object you have selected.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155338				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>searching; repeating a search\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155338				0	bs	Repeat Search				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155361				0	bs	With the \<emph\>Repeat search\</emph\> icon on the \<emph\>Navigation\</emph\> toolbar you can repeat a search you started with the \<emph\>Search and Replace\</emph\> dialog. To do this, click the icon. The blue arrow buttons on the vertical scrollbar now take on the functions \<emph\>Continue search forwards\</emph\> and \<emph\>Continue search backwards\</emph\>. If you now click one of the arrow surfaces, the search will be continued for the term entered in the Search and Replace dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_idN106DD				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Select this icon to browse through bookmarks.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
