helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155954				0	bs	Examples				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153792				0	bs	='Ms.'				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150948				0	bs	returns field names with the field content "Ms."				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150333				0	bs	LIKE 'g?ve'				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147332				0	bs	returns field names with field content such as "give" and "gave".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146062				0	bs	LIKE 'S*'				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155350				0	bs	returns data fields with field contents such as "Sun".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152883				0	bs	BETWEEN 10 AND 20				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159406				0	bs	returns field names with field content between the values 10 and 20. (The fields can be either text fields or number fields).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148765				0	bs	IN (1; 3; 5; 7)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149712				0	bs	returns field names with the values 1, 3, 5, 7. If the field name contains an item number, for example, you can create a query that returns the item having the specified number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152948				0	bs	NOT IN ('Smith')				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147279				0	bs	returns field names that do not contain "Smith".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157998				0	bs	\<emph\>Date fields\</emph\> are represented as #Date# to clearly identify them as dates. The date condition will be reproduced in the resulting SQL statement in the following ODBC - compliant way:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153734	253			0	bs	Date				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159131	254			0	bs	{D'YYYY-MM-DD'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153937	255			0	bs	Date time				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146090	256			0	bs	{D'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155947	257			0	bs	Time				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150964	258			0	bs	{D'HH:MM:SS'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151220	260			0	bs	$[officename] also supports the following \<emph\>Escape sequences\</emph\> known from ODBC and JDBC:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157975	261			0	bs	Date				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149753	262			0	bs	{d 'YYYY-MM-DD'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156318	263			0	bs	Time				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151280	264			0	bs	{t 'HH:MI:SS[.SS]'} - [ ] optional				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153264	265			0	bs	DateTime				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153981	266			0	bs	{ts 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MI:SS[.SS]'} - [ ] optional				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149539				0	bs	Example: select {d '1999-12-31'} from world.years				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146073	268			0	bs	\<emph\>Like \</emph\>Escape Sequence: {escape 'escape-character'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150661				0	bs	Example: select * from Item where ItemName like 'The *%' {escape '*'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148541				0	bs	The example will give you all of the entries where the item name begins with 'The *'. This means that you can also search for characters that would otherwise be interpreted as placeholders, such as *, ?, _, % or the period.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150572				0	bs	\<emph\>Outer Join\</emph\> Escape Sequence: {oj outer-join}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156052				0	bs	Example: select Article.* from {oj item LEFT OUTER JOIN orders ON item.no=orders.ANR}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153674				0	bs	Querying text fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149134				0	bs	To query the content of a text field, you must put the expression between single quotes. The distinction between uppercase and lowercase letters depends on the database in use. LIKE, by definition, is case-sensitive (though some databases don't see it that strict).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149302				0	bs	Querying date fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150510				0	bs	Even if you want to filter by a date, you must place the expression between single quotation marks. The following formats are valid: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS and YYYY/MM/DD HH:MM:SS as well as YYYY.MM.DD HH:MM:SS				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150427				0	bs	Querying Yes/No fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149523				0	bs	To query Yes/No fields, use the following syntax for dBase tables:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153180				0	bs	Status				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147481				0	bs	Query criterion				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155187				0	bs	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156092				0	bs	Yes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152414				0	bs	for dBase tables: not equal to any given value				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151265				0	bs	=1 returns all records where the Yes/No field has the status "Yes" or "On" (selected in black),				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152450				0	bs	No				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155331				0	bs	=0 returns all records for which the Yes/No field has the status "No" or "Off" (no selection).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154179				0	bs	Null				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147035				0	bs	IS NULL				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159385				0	bs	IS NULL returns all records for which the Yes/No field has neither of the states Yes or No (selected in gray).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157888				0	bs	The syntax depends on the database system used. You should also note that Yes/No fields can be defined differently (only 2 states instead of 3).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145772				0	bs	Parameter queries				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157312				0	bs	You must place the variable between square brackets (=[x]) to create a query with variable parameters. Alternatively, you can use an equal sign followed by a colon (=:x). When the query is executed, the program will display a dialog asking you for the expression to which the variable x should be assigned.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150818				0	bs	If you query several parameters at the same time, you will see a list field in the dialog containing all of the parameters and an input line alongside each one. Enter the values, preferably from top to bottom, and press the Enter key after each line.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157855				0	bs	Parameter queries with placeholders (*, _) or special characters (for example, ?) are not possible.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157537				0	bs	If you formulate a parameter query and you save it with the variables, you can later create a query in which only the variables have to be replaced by the expressions that you want. $[officename] asks for these variables in a dialog as soon as you open the query.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151035				0	bs	Parameter Input				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153596				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>The \<emph\>Parameter Input\</emph\> dialog asks you which variables you defined in the query. Enter a value for each query variable and confirm by clicking \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150585				0	bs	Parameter queries are also used for \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170203.xhp\" name=\"subforms\"\>subforms\</link\>, since they work exclusively with queries for which the values to be invoked are read internally from a variable.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153645				0	bs	A parameter query can have the following form in an SQL statement:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156731				0	bs	select * from 'addresses' where 'name' = :placeholder				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145181				0	bs	SQL Mode				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147013				0	bs	SQL stands for "Structured Query Language" and describes instructions for updating and administering relational databases.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152570	136			0	bs	In $[officename] you do not need any knowledge of SQL for most queries, since you do not have to enter the SQL code. If you create a query in the query design, $[officename] automatically converts your instructions into the corresponding SQL syntax. If, with the help of the \<emph\>Switch Design View On/Off \</emph\>button, you change to the SQL view, you can see the SQL commands for a query that has been created previously.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152412				0	bs	You can formulate your query directly in the SQL code. Note, however, that the special syntax is dependent upon the database system that you use.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146842				0	bs	If you enter the SQL code manually, you can create SQL-specific queries that are not supported by the graphical interface in \<emph\>Query design\</emph\>. These queries must be executed in native SQL mode.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149632				0	bs	By clicking the \<link href=\"text/shared/02/14030000.xhp\" name=\"Run SQL command directly\"\>\<emph\>Run SQL command directly\</emph\>\</link\> icon in the SQL view, you can formulate a query that is not processed by $[officename].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11000002.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Data sources in $[officename]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11000002.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155449				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>databases;drag and drop\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151299				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/11000002.xhp\" name=\"Data sources in $[officename]\"\>Data sources in $[officename]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150616				0	bs	Selecting the Address Book				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3153049				0	bs	To select the address book that you want to use, choose \<link href=\"text/shared/01/01110101.xhp\" name=\"File - Templates - Address Book Source\"\>\<emph\>File - Templates - Address Book Source\</emph\>\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11000002.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147275				0	bs	Opening a Data Source				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154143				0	bs	To open the data source view, press F4 in a text, spreadsheet or form document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11000002.xhp	0	help	par_id3154046				0	bs	To view the contents of a database, click the plus sign (+) in front it name in the data source view.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
