helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149566				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>ISLEAPYEAR function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149566				0	bs	ISLEAPYEAR				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148487				0	bs	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_id3150205				0	bs	ISLEAPYEAR(Date)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_id3159239				0	bs	Date: Specifies whether a given date falls within a leap year. The Date parameter must be a valid date according to the locale settings of %PRODUCTNAME.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149817				0	bs	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_id3150786				0	bs	ISLEAPYEAR(A1) returns 1, if A1 contains 2/29/68, the valid date 29th of February 1968 in your locale setting.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_idN107E7				0	bs	You may also use ISLEAPYEAR("2/29/68").				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	par_idN107EA				0	bs	Never use ISLEAPYEAR(2/29/68), because this would first evaluate 2 divided by 29 devided by 68, and then calculate the ISLEAPYEAR function from this small number as a serial date number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060111.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154656				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>YEARS function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
