helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3156014	383			0	bs	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\>: the date of purchase of the security.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154304	384			0	bs	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\>: the date on which the security matures (expires).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159180	385			0	bs	\<emph\>Price\</emph\>: The price of the security per 100 currency units of par value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3147253	386			0	bs	\<emph\>Redemption\</emph\>: the redemption value of the security per 100 currency units of par value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151174				0	bs	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155902				0	bs	A security is purchased on 1.25.2001; the maturity date is 11.15.2001. The price (purchase price) is 97, the redemption value is 100. Using daily balance calculation (basis 3) how high is the settlement (discount)?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3152797	389			0	bs	=DISC("1.25.2001"; "11.15.2001"; 97; 100; 3) returns 0.03840 or 3.84 per cent.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154695				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>DURATION_ADD function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154695				0	bs	DURATION_ADD				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145768				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_DURATION\"\>Calculates the duration of a fixed interest security in years.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153904				0	bs	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153373				0	bs	DURATION_ADD(Settlement;Maturity;Coupon;Yield;Frequency;Basis)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155397	406			0	bs	\<emph\>Settlement\</emph\>: the date of purchase of the security.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148558	407			0	bs	\<emph\>Maturity\</emph\>: the date on which the security matures (expires).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153096	408			0	bs	\<emph\>Coupon\</emph\>: the annual coupon interest rate (nominal rate of interest)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154594	409			0	bs	\<emph\>Yield\</emph\>: the annual yield of the security.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149906	410			0	bs	\<emph\>Frequency\</emph\>: number of interest payments per year (1, 2 or 4).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146995				0	bs	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148834				0	bs	A security is purchased on 1.1.2001; the maturity date is 1.1.2006. The Coupon rate of interest is 8%. The yield is 9.0%. Interest is paid half-yearly (frequency is 2). Using daily balance interest calculation (basis 3) how long is the duration?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154902	413			0	bs	=DURATION_ADD("1.1.2001"; "1.1.2006"; 0.08; 0.09; 2; 3)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159147				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>annual net interest rates\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; annual net interest rates\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>EFFECTIVE function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159147				0	bs	EFFECTIVE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154204				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_EFFEKTIV\"\>Returns the net annual interest rate for a nominal interest rate.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145417				0	bs	Nominal interest refers to the amount of interest due at the end of a calculation period. Effective interest increases with the number of payments made. In other words, interest is often paid in installments (for example, monthly or quarterly) before the end of the calculation period.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150510				0	bs	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148805				0	bs	EFFECTIVE(NOM;P)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149768	93			0	bs	\<emph\>NOM\</emph\> is the nominal interest.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149334	94			0	bs	\<emph\>P\</emph\> is the number of interest payment periods per year.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154223				0	bs	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3144499				0	bs	If the annual nominal interest rate is 9.75% and four interest calculation periods are defined, what is the actual interest rate (effective rate)?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150772	97			0	bs	EFFECTIVE(9.75%;4) = 10.11% The annual effective rate is therefore 10.11%.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147241				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>effective rates\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>EFFECT_ADD function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147241				0	bs	EFFECT_ADD				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3147524				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_AAI_FUNC_EFFECT\"\>Calculates the effective annual rate of interest on the basis of the nominal interest rate and the number of interest payments per annum.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155364				0	bs	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155118				0	bs	EFFECT_ADD(Nominal rate;Npery)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148907	418			0	bs	Nominal rate: the annual nominal rate of interest.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154274	419			0	bs	Npery: the number of interest payments per year.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149156				0	bs	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3158426				0	bs	What is the effective annual rate of interest for a 5.25% nominal rate and quarterly payment.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148927	422			0	bs	=EFFECT_ADD(0.0525; 4) returns 0.053543 or 5.3534%.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149998				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>calculating; arithmetic degressive depreciations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>arithmetic degressive depreciations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>DDB function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149998				0	bs	DDB				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159190				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GDA\"\>Returns the depreciation of an asset for a specified period using the arithmetic-declining method.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3152361				0	bs	Use this form of depreciation if you require a higher initial depreciation value as opposed to linear depreciation. The depreciation value gets less with each period and is usually used for assets whose value loss is higher shortly after purchase (for example, vehicles, computers). Please note that the book value will never reach zero under this calculation type.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156038				0	bs	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3166452				0	bs	DDB(Cost;Salvage;Life;Period;Factor)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153237	104			0	bs	\<emph\>Cost\</emph\> fixes the initial cost of an asset.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149787	105			0	bs	\<emph\>Salvage\</emph\> fixes the value of an asset at the end of its life.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3152945	106			0	bs	\<emph\>Life\</emph\> is the number of periods defining how long the asset is to be used.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149736	107			0	bs	\<emph\>Period\</emph\> defines the length of the period. The length must be entered in the same time unit as life.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150243	108			0	bs	\<emph\>Factor\</emph\> (optional) is the factor by which depreciation decreases. If a value is not entered, the default is factor 2.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159274				0	bs	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3152882				0	bs	A computer system with an initial cost of 75,000 currency units is to be depreciated monthly over 5 years. The value at the end of the depreciation is to be 1 currency unit. The factor is 2.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154106	111			0	bs	DDB(75000;1;60;12;2) = 1,721.81 currency units. Therefore, the double-declining depreciation during the first month after purchase is 1,721.81 currency units.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149962				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>calculating; geometric-degressive depreciations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>geometric-degressive depreciations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>DB function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149962				0	bs	DB				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148989				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GDA2\"\>Returns the depreciation of an asset for a specified period using the double-declining balance method.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3156213				0	bs	This form of depreciation is used if you want to get a higher depreciation value at the beginning of the depreciation (as opposed to linear depreciation). The depreciation value is reduced with every depreciation period by the depreciation already deducted from the initial cost.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149807				0	bs	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153349				0	bs	DB(Cost;Salvage;Life;Period;Month)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148462	118			0	bs	\<emph\>Cost\</emph\> is the initial cost of an asset.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148658	119			0	bs	\<emph\>Salvage\</emph\> is the value of an asset at the end of the depreciation.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145371	120			0	bs	\<emph\>Life\</emph\> Life defines the period over which an asset is depreciated.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154608	121			0	bs	\<emph\>Period\</emph\> is the length of each period. The length must be entered in the same date unit as the depreciation period.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150829	122			0	bs	\<emph\>Month\</emph\> (optional) denotes the number of months for the first year of depreciation. If an entry is not defined, 12 is used as the default.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151130				0	bs	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3156147				0	bs	A computer system with an initial cost of 25,000 currency units is to be depreciated over a three year period. The salvage value is to be 1,000 currency units. One period is 30 days.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149513	125			0	bs	DB(25000;1000;36;1;6) = 1,075.00 currency units				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159242				0	bs	The fixed-declining depreciation of the computer system is 1,075.00 currency units.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153948				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>IRR function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating; internal rates\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>internal rates\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153948				0	bs	IRR				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3143282				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_IKV\"\>Calculates the internal rate of return for an investment.\</ahelp\> The values represent cash flow values at regular intervals, at least one value must be negative (payments), and at least one value must be positive (income).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150599				0	bs	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3155427				0	bs	IRR(Values;Guess)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3144758	132			0	bs	\<emph\>Values\</emph\> represents an array containing the values.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149233	133			0	bs	\<emph\>Guess\</emph\> (optional) is the estimated value. An iterative method is used to calculate the internal rate of return. If you can provide only few values, you should provide an initial guess to enable the iteration.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151258				0	bs	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150630				0	bs	Under the assumption that cell contents are A1=-10000, A2=3500, A3=7600 and A4=1000, the formula =IRR(A1:A4) gives a result of 80.24%.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151012				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>calculating; interests for unchanged amortization installments\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>interests for unchanged amortization installments\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>ISPMT function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151012				0	bs	ISPMT				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148693				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_ISPMT\"\>Calculates the level of interest for unchanged amortization installments.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154661				0	bs	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3146070				0	bs	ISPMT(Rate; Period; Total_periods; Invest)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3148672	318			0	bs	\<emph\>Rate\</emph\> sets the periodic interest rate.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3145777	319			0	bs	\<emph\>Period\</emph\> is the number of installments for calculation of interest.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3153678	320			0	bs	\<emph\>Total_periods\</emph\> is the total number of installment periods.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3159390	321			0	bs	\<emph\>Invest\</emph\> is the amount of the investment.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156162				0	bs	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3149558				0	bs	For a credit amount of 120,000 currency units with a two-year term and monthly installments, at a yearly interest rate of 12% the level of interest after 1.5 years is required.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3150949	324			0	bs	ISPMT(1%;18;24;120000) = -300 currency units. The monthly interest after 1.5 years amounts to 300 currency units.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3146812				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060119.xhp\" name=\"Forward to Financial Functions Part Two\"\>Financial Functions Part Two\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060103.xhp	0	help	par_id3154411				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060118.xhp\" name=\"Forward to Financial Functions Part Three\"\>Financial Functions Part Three\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_minute.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	MINUTE 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_minute.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149803				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>MINUTE function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_minute.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149803				0	bs	\<variable id=\"minute\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/func_minute.xhp\"\>MINUTE\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_minute.xhp	0	help	par_id3148988				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_MINUTE\"\>Calculates the minute for an internal time value\</ahelp\> The minute is returned as a number between 0 and 59.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_minute.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154343				0	bs	Syntax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_minute.xhp	0	help	par_id3148660				0	bs	MINUTE(number)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_minute.xhp	0	help	par_id3154611				0	bs	\<emph\>Number\</emph\>, as a time value, is a decimal number where the number of the minute is to be returned.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_minute.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145374				0	bs	Examples				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_minute.xhp	0	help	par_id3148463				0	bs	MINUTE(8.999) returns 58				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_minute.xhp	0	help	par_id3149419				0	bs	MINUTE(8.9999) returns 59				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_minute.xhp	0	help	par_id3144755				0	bs	MINUTE(NOW()) returns the current minute value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\05020600.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Cell Protection				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\05020600.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145119				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05020600.xhp\" name=\"Cell Protection\"\>Cell Protection\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\05020600.xhp	0	help	par_id3150398				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SCPAGE_PROTECTION\"\>Defines protection options for selected cells.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\05020600.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150447				0	bs	Protection				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\05020600.xhp	0	help	hd_id3125864				0	bs	Hide all				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\05020600.xhp	0	help	par_id3153768				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:TRISTATEBOX:RID_SCPAGE_PROTECTION:BTN_HIDE_ALL\"\>Hides formulas and contents of the selected cells.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\05020600.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153190				0	bs	Protected				2002-02-02 02:02:02
