helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Inserting External Data in Table (WebQuery)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154346				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>HTML WebQuery\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>ranges; inserting in tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>external data; inserting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables; inserting external data\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>web pages; importing data\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>WebQuery filter\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>external data;WebQuery\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting; external data\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>data sources; external data\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	hd_id3125863				0	bs	\<variable id=\"webquery\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/webquery.xhp\" name=\"Inserting External Data in Table (WebQuery)\"\>Inserting External Data in Table (WebQuery)\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3155131				0	bs	With the help of the \<emph\>Web Page Query ($[officename] Calc)\</emph\> import filter, you can insert tables from HTML documents in a Calc spreadsheet.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3148575				0	bs	You can use the same method to insert ranges defined by name from a Calc or Microsoft Excel spreadsheet.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3149664				0	bs	The following insert methods are available:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146976				0	bs	Inserting by Dialog				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	bs	Set the cell cursor at the cell where the new content will be inserted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3145750				0	bs	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Link to External Data\</emph\>. This opens the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04090000.xhp\"\>External Data\</link\> dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3149958				0	bs	Enter the URL of the HTML document or the name of the spreadsheet. Press Enter when finished. Click the \<emph\>...\</emph\> button to open a file selection dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3149400				0	bs	In the large list box of the dialog, select the named ranges or tables you want to insert.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3155064				0	bs	You can also specify that the ranges or tables are updated every n seconds.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443				0	bs	The import filter can create names for cell ranges on the fly. As much formatting as possible is retained, while the filter intentionally does not load any images.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149021				0	bs	Inserting by Navigator				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3153965				0	bs	Open two documents: the $[officename] Calc spreadsheet in which the external data is to be inserted (target document) and the document from which the external data derives (source document).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3150205				0	bs	In the target document open the Navigator.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3152990				0	bs	In the lower combo box of the Navigator select the source document. The Navigator now shows the range names and database ranges or the tables contained in the source document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3148842	20			0	bs	In the Navigator select the \<emph\>Insert as link\</emph\> drag mode \<image id=\"img_id3152985\" src=\"sw/imglst/sc20238.png\" width=\"0.222inch\" height=\"0.222inch\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3152985\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3157978				0	bs	Drag the desired external data from the Navigator into the target document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3144768				0	bs	If you have loaded an HTML document with the \<emph\>Web Page Query\</emph\> filter as the source document, you will find the tables in the Navigator, named continuously from "HTML_table1" onwards, and also two range names that have been created:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3152873				0	bs	\<item type=\"literal\"\>HTML_all\</item\> - designates the entire document				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3149897				0	bs	\<item type=\"literal\"\>HTML_tables\</item\> - designates all HTML tables in the document				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149126				0	bs	Editing the external data				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3159228				0	bs	Open \<emph\>Edit - Links\</emph\>. Here you can edit the link to the external data.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\webquery.xhp	0	help	par_id3154650				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04090000.xhp\" name=\"External data dialog\"\>External data dialog\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_table.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Formatting Spreadsheets				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_table.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154125				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>text in cells; formatting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>spreadsheets;formatting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>backgrounds;cells and pages\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>borders;cells and pages\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting;spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_table.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154125				0	bs	\<variable id=\"format_table\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/format_table.xhp\" name=\"Designing Spreadsheets\"\>Formatting Spreadsheets\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_table.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153912	13			0	bs	Formatting Text in a Spreadsheet 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_table.xhp	0	help	par_id3144772				0	bs	Select the text you want to format.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_table.xhp	0	help	par_id3155268	15			0	bs	Choose the desired text attributes from the \<emph\>Formatting \</emph\>Bar. You can also choose \<emph\>Format - Cells\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog will appear in which you can choose various text attributes on the \<emph\>Font\</emph\> tab page.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_table.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149899				0	bs	Formatting Numbers in a Spreadsheet				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_table.xhp	0	help	par_id3159226				0	bs	Select the cells containing the numbers you want to format.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_table.xhp	0	help	par_id3150046	18			0	bs	To format numbers in the default currency format or as percentages, use the icons on the \<emph\>Formatting \</emph\>Bar. For other formats, choose \<emph\>Format - Cells\</emph\>. You can choose from the preset formats or define your own on the \<emph\>Numbers\</emph\> tab page.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_table.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153483				0	bs	Formatting Borders and Backgrounds for Cells and Pages				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_table.xhp	0	help	par_id3145116				0	bs	To apply formatting attributes to an entire sheet, choose \<emph\>Format - Page\</emph\>. You can define headers and footers, for example, to appear on each printed page.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\format_table.xhp	0	help	par_id3145389				0	bs	An image that you have loaded with \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Page - Background\</item\> is only visible in print or in the page preview. To display a background image on screen as well, insert the graphic image by choosing \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Picture - From File\</item\> and arrange the image behind the cells by choosing \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Arrange - To Background\</item\>. Use the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/02110000.xhp\" name=\"Navigator\"\>Navigator\</link\> to select the background image.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Freezing Rows or Columns as Headers				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154684				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>tables; freezing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>title rows; freezing during table split\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rows; freezing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>columns; freezing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>freezing rows or columns\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>headers; freezing during table split\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>scrolling prevention in tables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>windows; splitting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables; splitting windows\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154684				0	bs	\<variable id=\"line_fix\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/line_fix.xhp\" name=\"Freezing Rows or Columns as Headers\"\>Freezing Rows or Columns as Headers\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3148576				0	bs	If you have long rows or columns of data that extend beyond the viewable area of the spreadsheet, you can freeze them, which allows you to see the columns or rows as you scroll through the rest of the data.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3156441				0	bs	Select the row below, or the column to the right of the row or column that you want to be in the frozen region. All rows above, or all columns to the left of the selection are frozen.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3153158				0	bs	To freeze both horizontally and vertically, select the \<emph\>cell\</emph\> that is below the row and to the right of the column that you want to freeze.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3156286				0	bs	Choose \<emph\>Window - Freeze\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3151073	5			0	bs	To deactivate, choose \<emph\>Window - Freeze \</emph\>again.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335				0	bs	If the area defined is to be scrollable, apply the \<emph\>Window - Split\</emph\> command.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3147345				0	bs	If you want to print a certain row on all pages of a document, choose \<emph\>Format - Print ranges - Edit\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3147004				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/07090000.xhp\" name=\"Window - Freeze\"\>Window - Freeze\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/07080000.xhp\" name=\"Window - Split\"\>Window - Split\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\line_fix.xhp	0	help	par_id3150304				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05080300.xhp\" name=\"Format - Print ranges - Edit\"\>Format - Print ranges - Edit\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Printing Rows or Columns on Every Page				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151112				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>printing; sheets on multiple pages\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sheets; printing on multiple pages\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>rows; repeating when printing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>columns; repeating when printing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>fixed rows or columns; printing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>repeating;columns/rows on printed pages\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>title rows; printing on all sheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>headers; printing on sheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>footers; printing on sheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>printing; rows/columns as table headings\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153727				0	bs	\<variable id=\"print_title_row\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/print_title_row.xhp\" name=\"Printing Rows or Columns on Every Page\"\>Printing Rows or Columns on Every Page\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3154014				0	bs	If you have a sheet that is so large that it will be printed multiple pages, you can set up rows or columns to repeat on each printed page.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975				0	bs	As an example, If you want to print the top two rows of the sheet as well as the first column (A)on all pages, do the following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3163710				0	bs	Choose \<emph\>Format - Print Ranges - Edit\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Edit Print Ranges\</emph\> dialog appears.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3149958				0	bs	Click the icon at the far right of the \<emph\>Rows to repeat\</emph\> area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3145800				0	bs	The dialog shrinks so that you can see more of the sheet.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3155602				0	bs	Select the first two rows and, for this example, click cell A1 and drag to A2.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3154018				0	bs	In the shrunk dialog you will see $1:$2. Rows 1 and 2 are now rows to repeat.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3153707				0	bs	Click the icon at the far right of the \<emph\>Rows to repeat\</emph\> area. The dialog is restored again.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443				0	bs	If you also want column A as a column to repeat, click the icon at the far right of the \<emph\>Columns to repeat\</emph\> area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3154256				0	bs	Click column A (not in the column header).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3154704				0	bs	Click the icon again at the far right of the \<emph\>Columns to repeat\</emph\> area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088				0	bs	Rows to repeat are rows from the sheet. You can define headers and footers to be printed on each print page independently of this in \<emph\>Format - Page\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3155380				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/03100000.xhp\" name=\"View - Page Break Preview\"\>View - Page Break Preview\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3154371				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05080300.xhp\" name=\"Format - Print ranges - Edit\"\>Format - Print ranges - Edit\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_title_row.xhp	0	help	par_id3146113				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05070000.xhp\" name=\"Format - Page - (Header / Footer)\"\>Format - Page - (Header / Footer)\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Inserting and Editing Notes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153968				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>notes; on cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells;notes\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>comments; on cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>remarks; on cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting;notes on cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>viewing;notes on cells\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153968				0	bs	\<variable id=\"note_insert\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/note_insert.xhp\" name=\"Inserting and Editing Notes\"\>Inserting and Editing Notes\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3150440				0	bs	You can assign a note to each cell by choosing \<link href=\"text/shared/01/04050000.xhp\" name=\"Insert - Note\"\>\<emph\>Insert - Note\</emph\>\</link\>. The note is indicated by a small red square, the note indicator, in the cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3145750				0	bs	The note is visible whenever the mouse pointer is over the cell, provided you have activated \<emph\>Help - Tips\</emph\> or - \<emph\>Extended Tips\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3148575				0	bs	When you select the cell, you can choose \<emph\>Show Note\</emph\> from the context menu of the cell. Doing so keeps the note visible until you deactivate the \<emph\>Show Note\</emph\> command from the same context menu.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3149958				0	bs	To edit a permanently visible note, just click in it. If you delete the entire text of the note, the note itself is deleted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_idN10699				0	bs	Move or resize each note as you like.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_idN1069D				0	bs	Format each note by specifying background color, transparency, border style, and text alignment. Choose the commands from the context menu of the note.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715				0	bs	To display a help tip for a selected cell, use \<emph\>Data - Validity - Input Help\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\note_insert.xhp	0	help	par_id3153707				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/04050000.xhp\" name=\"Insert - Note\"\>Insert - Note\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Selecting Themes for Sheets				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150791				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>theme selection for sheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>layout of spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell styles; selecting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>selecting;formatting themes\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>applying;formatting themes\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sheets;formatting themes\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formats;themes for sheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting;themes for sheets\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150791	6			0	bs	\<variable id=\"design\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/design.xhp\" name=\"Selecting Themes for Sheets\"\>Selecting Themes for Sheets\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	par_id3145786				0	bs	$[officename] Calc comes with a predefined set of formatting themes that you can apply to your spreadsheets.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490				0	bs	It is not possible to add themes to Calc, and they cannot be modified. However, you can modify their styles after you apply them to a spreadsheet.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	par_id3154757				0	bs	Before you format a sheet with a theme, you have to apply at least one custom cell style to the cells on the sheet. You can then change the cell formatting by selecting and applying a theme in the \<emph\>Theme Selection\</emph\> dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	par_id3156382				0	bs	To apply a custom cell style to a cell, you can open the Styles and Formatting window and, in its lower list box, set the Custom Styles view. A list of the existing custom defined cell styles will be displayed. Double click a name from the Styles and Formatting window to apply this style to the selected cells.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	par_id3153963				0	bs	To apply a theme to a spreadsheet:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	par_id3146920	15			0	bs	Click the \<emph\>Choose Themes\</emph\> icon in the \<emph\>Tools\</emph\> bar. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	par_id3148488				0	bs	The \<emph\>Theme Selection\</emph\> dialog appears. This dialog lists the available themes for the whole spreadsheet and the Styles and Formatting window lists the custom styles for specific cells.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	par_id3155114	9			0	bs	In the \<emph\>Theme Selection \</emph\>dialog, select the theme that you want to apply to the spreadsheet.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	par_id3150090				0	bs	Click OK				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	par_id3150201				0	bs	As soon as you select another theme in the \<emph\>Theme Selection\</emph\> dialog, some of the properties of the custom style will be applied to the current spreadsheet. The modifications will be immediately visible in your spreadsheet.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\design.xhp	0	help	par_id3146979				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/02/06080000.xhp\" name=\"Theme selection\"\>Theme selection\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\datapilot_edittable.xhp	0	help	par_id3145786				0	bs	By dragging the button to a different position in the same row you can alter the order of the columns. If you drag a button to the left edge of the table into the row headings area, you can change a column into a row.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Entering Fractions 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155411				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>fractions; entering\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>numbers; entering fractions \</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;fractions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155411				0	bs	\<variable id=\"fraction_enter\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/fraction_enter.xhp\" name=\"Entering Fractions \"\>Entering Fractions \</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153968				0	bs	You can enter a fractional number in a cell and use it for calculation:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3155133				0	bs	Enter "0 1/5" in a cell (without the quotation marks) and press the input key. In the input line above the spreadsheet you will see the value 0.2, which is used for the calculation.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3145750				0	bs	If you enter "0 1/2" AutoCorrect causes the three characters 1, / and 2 to be replaced by a single character. The same applies to 1/4 and 3/4. This replacement is defined in \<emph\>Tools - AutoCorrect\</emph\> - \<emph\>Replace\</emph\> tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\fraction_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3145367	44			0	bs	If you want to see multi-digit fractions such as "1/10", you must change the cell format to the multi-digit fraction view. Open the context menu of the cell, and choose \<emph\>Format cells. \</emph\>Select "Fraction" from the \<emph\>Category\</emph\> field, and then select "-1234 10/81". You can then enter fractions such as 12/31 or 12/32 - the fractions are, however, automatically reduced, so that in the last example you would see 3/8.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Applying Conditional Formatting				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149263				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>conditional formatting; cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; conditional formatting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting; conditional formatting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>styles;conditional styles\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formats; conditional\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell formats; conditional\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>random numbers; generating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell styles; copying\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>copying; cell styles\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>tables; copying cell styles\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149263				0	bs	\<variable id=\"cellstyle_conditional\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellstyle_conditional.xhp\" name=\"Applying Conditional Formatting\"\>Applying Conditional Formatting\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3159156				0	bs	Using the menu command \<emph\>Format - Conditional formatting\</emph\>, the dialog allows you to define up to three conditions per cell, which must be met in order for the selected cells to have a particular format.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3154944				0	bs	With conditional formatting, you can, for example, highlight the totals that exceed the average value of all totals. If the totals change, the formatting changes correspondingly, without having to apply other styles manually.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490				0	bs	Select the cells to which you want to apply a conditional style.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3155603				0	bs	Choose \<emph\>Format - Conditional Formatting\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3146969				0	bs	Enter the condition(s) into the dialog box. The dialog is described in detail in \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05120000.xhp\" name=\"$[officename] Help\"\>$[officename] Help\</link\>, and an example is provided below:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155766				0	bs	Example of Conditional Formatting: Generate Number Values				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3150043				0	bs	You want to give certain values in your tables particular emphasis. For example, in a table of turnovers, you can show all the values above the average in green and all those below the average in red. This is possible with conditional formatting.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3155337				0	bs	First of all, write a table in which a few different values occur. For your test you can create tables with any random numbers:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3149565				0	bs	In one of the cells enter the formula =RAND(), and you will obtain a random number between 0 and 1. If you want integers of between 0 and 50, enter the formula =INT(RAND()*50).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3149258				0	bs	Copy the formula to create a row of random numbers. Click the bottom right corner of the selected cell, and drag to the right until the desired cell range is selected.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3159236				0	bs	In the same way as described above, drag down the corner of the rightmost cell in order to create more rows of random numbers.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149211				0	bs	Example of Conditional Formatting: Define Cell Styles				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3154659				0	bs	The next step is to apply a cell style to all values that represent above-average turnover, and one to those that are below the average. Ensure that the Styles and Formatting window is visible before proceeding.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3150883				0	bs	Click in a blank cell and select the command \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> in the context menu.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3155529				0	bs	In the \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog on the \<emph\>Background\</emph\> tab, select a background color. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3154484				0	bs	In the Styles and Formatting window, click the \<emph\>New Style from Selection\</emph\> icon. Enter the name of the new style. For this example, name the style "Above".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3152889				0	bs	To define a second style, click again in a blank cell and proceed as described above. Assign a different background color for the cell and assign a name (for this example, "Below").				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148704				0	bs	Example of Conditional Formatting: Calculate Average				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3148837				0	bs	In our particular example, we are calculating the average of the random values. The result is placed in a cell:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3144768				0	bs	Set the cursor in a blank cell, for example, J14, and choose \<emph\>Insert - Function\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3156016				0	bs	Select the AVERAGE function. Use the mouse to select all your random numbers. If you cannot see the entire range, because Function Wizard is obscuring it, you can temporarily shrink the dialog using the \<link href=\"text/shared/00/00000001.xhp#eingabesymbol\" name=\"Shrink or Maximize\"\>Shrink / Maximize\</link\> icon.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3153246				0	bs	Close Function Wizard with \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149898				0	bs	Example of Conditional Formatting: Apply Cell Style				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3149126				0	bs	Now you can apply the conditional formatting to the sheet:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3150049				0	bs	Select all cells with the random numbers.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3153801				0	bs	Choose the \<emph\>Format - Conditional Formatting\</emph\> command to open the corresponding dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3153013				0	bs	Define the condition as follows: If cell value is less than J14, format with cell style "Below", and if cell value is greater than or equal to J14, format with cell style "Above".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155761				0	bs	Example of Conditional Formatting: Copy Cell Style				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3145320				0	bs	To apply the conditional formatting to other cells later:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3153074				0	bs	Click one of the cells that has been assigned conditional formatting.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3149051				0	bs	Copy the cell to the clipboard.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3150436				0	bs	Select the cells that are to receive this same formatting.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3147298				0	bs	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Paste Special\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Paste Special\</emph\> dialog appears.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3166465				0	bs	In the \<emph\>Selection\</emph\> area, check only the \<emph\>Formats\</emph\> box. All other boxes must be unchecked. Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_conditional.xhp	0	help	par_id3159123				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/05120000.xhp\" name=\"Format - Conditional formatting\"\>Format - Conditional formatting\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Addresses and References, Absolute and Relative				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	bm_id3156423				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>addressing; relative and absolute\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>references; absolute/relative\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>absolute addresses in spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>relative addresses\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>absolute references in spreadsheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>relative references\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>references; to cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; references\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156423				0	bs	\<variable id=\"relativ_absolut_ref\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/relativ_absolut_ref.xhp\" name=\"Addresses and References, Absolute and Relative\"\>Addresses and References, Absolute and Relative\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163712				0	bs	Relative Addressing				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119				0	bs	The cell in column A, row 1 is addressed as A1. You can address a range of adjacent cells by first entering the coordinates of the upper left cell of the area, then a colon followed by the coordinates of the lower right cell. For example, the square formed by the first four cells in the upper left corner is addressed as A1:B2.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3154730				0	bs	By addressing an area in this way, you are making a relative reference to A1:B2. Relative here means that the reference to this area will be adjusted automatically when you copy the formulas.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149377				0	bs	Absolute Addressing				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3154943				0	bs	Absolute references are the opposite of relative addressing. A dollar sign is placed before each letter and number in an absolute reference, for example, $A$1:$B$2.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3147338				0	bs	$[officename] can convert the current reference, in which the cursor is positioned in the input line, from relative to absolute and vice versa by pressing Shift +F4. If you start with a relative address such as A1, the first time you press this key combination, both row and column are set to absolute references ($A$1). The second time, only the row (A$1), and the third time, only the column ($A1). If you press the key combination once more, both column and row references are switched back to relative (A1)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3153963				0	bs	$[officename] Calc shows the references to a formula. If, for example you click the formula =SUM(A1:C5;D15:D24) in a cell, the two referenced areas in the sheet will be highlighted in color. For example, the formula component "A1:C5" may be in blue and the cell range in question bordered in the same shade of blue. The next formula component "D15:D24" can be marked in red in the same way.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154704				0	bs	When to Use Relative and Absolute References				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3147346				0	bs	What distinguishes a relative reference? Assume you want to calculate in cell E1 the sum of the cells in range A1:B2. The formula to enter into E1 would be: =SUM(A1:B2). If you later decide to insert a new column in front of column A, the elements you want to add would then be in B1:C2 and the formula would be in F1, not in E1. After inserting the new column, you would therefore have to check and correct all formulas in the sheet, and possibly in other sheets.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335				0	bs	Fortunately, $[officename] does this work for you. After having inserted a new column A, the formula =SUM(A1:B2) will be automatically updated to =SUM(B1:C2). Row numbers will also be automatically adjusted when a new row 1 is inserted. Absolute and relative references are always adjusted in $[officename] Calc whenever the referenced area is moved. But be careful if you are copying a formula since in that case only the relative references will be adjusted, not the absolute references.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3145791				0	bs	Absolute references are used when a calculation refers to one specific cell in your sheet. If a formula that refers to exactly this cell is copied relatively to a cell below the original cell, the reference will also be moved down if you did not define the cell coordinates as absolute.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\relativ_absolut_ref.xhp	0	help	par_id3147005				0	bs	Aside from when new rows and columns are inserted, references can also change when an existing formula referring to particular cells is copied to another area of the sheet. Assume you entered the formula =SUM(A1:A9) in row 10. If you want to calculate the sum for the adjacent column to the right, simply copy this formula to the cell to the right. The copy of the formula in column B will be automatically adjusted to =SUM(B1:B9).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Printing Sheet Details				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154346				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>printing;sheet details\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sheets; printing details\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>grids; printing sheet grids\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas; printing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>notes; printing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>charts;printing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sheet grids; printing\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154346				0	bs	\<variable id=\"print_details\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/print_details.xhp\" name=\"Printing Sheet Details\"\>Printing Sheet Details\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3153728				0	bs	When printing a sheet you can select which details are to be printed:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3150010				0	bs	Row and column headers				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3154013				0	bs	Sheet grid				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3145273				0	bs	Notes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3145801				0	bs	Objects and graphics				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3154491				0	bs	Charts				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3154731				0	bs	Drawing objects				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3149400				0	bs	Formulas				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3150752				0	bs	To choose the details proceed as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640				0	bs	Select the sheet you want to print.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3150042				0	bs	Choose \<emph\>Format - Page\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3147340	13			0	bs	The command is not visible if the sheet was opened with write protection on. In that case, click the \<emph\>Edit File \</emph\>icon on the \<emph\>Standard\</emph\> Bar.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3146916	14			0	bs	Select the \<emph\>Sheet\</emph\> tab. In the \<emph\>Print \</emph\>area mark the details to be printed and click OK.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3145789				0	bs	Print the document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_details.xhp	0	help	par_id3150345				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/03100000.xhp\" name=\"View - Page Break Preview\"\>View - Page Break Preview\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	User-Defined Functions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155411				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>functions; user-defined\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>user-defined functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Basic IDE; applying\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>IDE; Basic IDE\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>programming;functions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155411				0	bs	\<variable id=\"userdefined_function\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/userdefined_function.xhp\" name=\"Defining Functions Yourself\"\>User-Defined Functions\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3153969				0	bs	You can apply user-defined functions in $[officename] Calc in the following ways:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3145366				0	bs	You can define your own functions using the Basic-IDE. This method requires a basic knowledge of programming.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3153768				0	bs	You can program functions as \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060111.xhp\" name=\"add-ins\"\>add-ins\</link\>. This method requires an advanced knowledge of programming.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149260				0	bs	Defining A Function Using %PRODUCTNAME Basic				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148456				0	bs	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Macros - Organize Macros - %PRODUCTNAME Basic\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3154510				0	bs	Click the \<emph\>Edit\</emph\> button. You will now see the Basic IDE.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150327				0	bs	Enter the function code. In this example, we define a \<item type=\"literal\"\>VOL(a; b; c)\</item\> function that calculates the volume of a rectangular solid with side lengths \<item type=\"literal\"\>a\</item\>, \<item type=\"literal\"\>b\</item\> and \<item type=\"literal\"\>c\</item\>:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3155443				0	bs	Close the Basic-IDE window.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150043				0	bs	Your function is automatically saved in the default module and is now available. If you apply the function in a Calc document that is to be used on another computer, you can copy the function to the Calc document as described in the next section.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147340				0	bs	Copying a Function To a Document				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3145232	19			0	bs	In stage 2 of "Defining A Function Using %PRODUCTNAME Basic", in the \<emph\>Macro\</emph\> dialog you clicked on \<emph\>Edit \</emph\>. As the default, in the \<emph\>Macro from\</emph\> field the \<emph\>My Macros - Standard - Module1\</emph\> module is selected. The \<emph\>Standard\</emph\> library resides locally in your user directory.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3154022				0	bs	If you want to copy the user-defined function to a Calc document:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150304				0	bs	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Macros - Organize Macros - %PRODUCTNAME Basic\</item\> .				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150086				0	bs	In the \<emph\>Macro from\</emph\> field select \<emph\>My Macros - Standard - Module1\</emph\> and click \<emph\>Edit\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3166430				0	bs	In the Basic-IDE, select the source of your user-defined function and copy it to the clipboard.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_idN1081D				0	bs	Close the Basic-IDE.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3150517				0	bs	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Macros - Organize Macros - %PRODUCTNAME Basic\</item\> .				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3145384				0	bs	In the \<emph\>Macro from\</emph\> field select \<emph\>(Name of the Calc document) - Standard - Module1\</emph\>. Click \<emph\>Edit\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148699				0	bs	Paste the clipboard contents in the Basic-IDE of the document.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153305				0	bs	Applying a User-defined Function in $[officename] Calc				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148869				0	bs	Once you have defined the function \<item type=\"literal\"\>VOL(a; b; c)\</item\> in the Basic-IDE, you can apply it the same way as the built-in functions of $[officename] Calc.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3148606				0	bs	Open a Calc document and enter numbers for the function parameters \<item type=\"literal\"\>a\</item\>, \<item type=\"literal\"\>b\</item\>, and \<item type=\"literal\"\>c\</item\> in cells A1, B1, and C1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3156019				0	bs	Set the cursor in another cell and enter the following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3155264				0	bs	=VOL(A1;B1;C1)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\userdefined_function.xhp	0	help	par_id3146776				0	bs	The function is evaluated and you will see the result in the selected cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Defining Number of Pages for Printing				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153194				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>printing; sheet counts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sheets; printing sheet counts\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>page breaks; spreadsheet preview\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>editing;print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>viewing;print ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>previews;page breaks for printing\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153194				0	bs	\<variable id=\"print_exact\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/print_exact.xhp\" name=\"Defining Number of Pages for Printing\"\>Defining Number of Pages for Printing\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	par_id3153771				0	bs	If a sheet is too large for a single printed page, $[officename] Calc will print the current sheet evenly divided over several pages. Since the automatic page break does not always take place in the optimal position, you can define the page distribution yourself.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	par_id3159155				0	bs	Go to the sheet to be printed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	par_id3150012				0	bs	Choose \<emph\>View - Page Break Preview\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	par_id3146974				0	bs	You will see the automatic distribution of the sheet across the print pages. The automatically created print ranges are indicated by dark blue lines, and the user-defined ones by light blue lines. The page breaks (line breaks and column breaks) are marked as black lines.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	par_id3152578				0	bs	You can move the blue lines with the mouse. You will find further options in the Context menu, including adding an additional print range, removing the scaling and inserting additional manual line and column breaks.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\print_exact.xhp	0	help	par_id3151073				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/03100000.xhp\" name=\"View - Page Break Preview\"\>View - Page Break Preview\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Calculating With Dates and Times				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146120				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>dates; in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>times; in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells;date and time formats\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>current date and time values\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146120				0	bs	\<variable id=\"calc_date\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_date.xhp\" name=\"Calculating With Dates and Times\"\>Calculating With Dates and Times\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3154320				0	bs	In $[officename] Calc, you can perform calculations with current date and time values. As an example, to find out exactly how old you are in seconds or hours, follow the following steps:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3150750				0	bs	In a spreadsheet, enter your birthday in cell A1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3145642	14			0	bs	Enter the following formula in cell A3: =NOW()-A1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3149020				0	bs	After pressing the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key you will see the result in date format. Since the result should show the difference between two dates as a number of days, you must format cell A3 as a number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3155335				0	bs	Place the cursor in cell A3, right-click to open a context menu and choose \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3147343				0	bs	The \<emph\>Format Cells\</emph\> dialog appears. On the \<emph\>Numbers\</emph\> tab, the \<emph\>Number\</emph\> category will appear already highlighted. The format is set to "General", which causes the result of a calculation containing date entries to be displayed as a date. To display the result as a number, set the number format to "-1,234" and close the dialog with the OK button.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3147001				0	bs	The number of days between today's date and the specified date is displayed in cell A3.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3150304				0	bs	Experiment with some additional formulas: in A4 enter =A3*24 to calculate the hours, in A5 enter =A4*60 for the minutes, and in A6 enter =A5*60 for seconds. Press the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key after each formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3149207				0	bs	The time since your date of birth will be calculated and displayed in the various units. The values are calculated as of the exact moment when you entered the last formula and pressed the \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> key. This value is not automatically updated, although "Now" continuously changes. In the \<emph\>Tools\</emph\> menu, the menu item \<emph\>Cell Contents - AutoCalculate\</emph\> is normally active; however, automatic calculation does not apply to the function NOW. This ensures that your computer is not solely occupied with updating the sheet.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Entering Formulas in the Formula Bar				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150868				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>formula bar; input line\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>input line in formula bar\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas; inputting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;formulas\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150868				0	bs	\<variable id=\"formula_enter\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/formula_enter.xhp\" name=\"Entering Formulas in the Formula Bar\"\>Entering Formulas in the Formula Bar\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3145364				0	bs	Click the cell in which you want to enter the formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3150012				0	bs	Click the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> icon on the Formula Bar.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3156441				0	bs	You will now see an equals sign in the input line and you can begin to input the formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3153726				0	bs	After entering the required values, press Enter or click \<emph\>Accept\</emph\> to insert the result in the active cell. If you want to clear your entry in the input line, press Escape or click \<emph\>Cancel\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3147394				0	bs	You can also enter the values and the formulas directly into the cells, even if you cannot see an input cursor. Formulas must always begin with an equals sign.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3149210	7			0	bs	\<variable id=\"tip\"\>If you would like to view the calculation of individual elements of a formula, select the respective elements and press F9. For example, in the formula =SUM(A1:B12)*SUM(C1:D12) select the section SUM(C1:D12) and press F9 to view the subtotal for this area. \</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3150304				0	bs	If an error occurs when creating the formula, an \<link href=\"text/scalc/05/02140000.xhp\" name=\"error message\"\>error message\</link\> appears in the active cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_enter.xhp	0	help	par_id3152993				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/main0206.xhp\" name=\"Formula bar\"\>Formula bar\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Calculating Time Differences				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150769				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>calculating;time differences\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>time differences\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150769				0	bs	\<variable id=\"calc_timevalues\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/calc_timevalues.xhp\" name=\"Calculating Time Differences\"\>Calculating Time Differences\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3149263				0	bs	If you want to calculate time differences, for example, the time between 23:30 and 01:10 in the same night, use the following formula:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3159153				0	bs	=(B2<A2)+B2-A2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3152598				0	bs	The later time is B2 and the earlier time is A2. The result of the example is 01:40 or 1 hour and 40 minutes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\calc_timevalues.xhp	0	help	par_id3145271				0	bs	In the formula, an entire 24-hour day has a value of 1 and one hour has a value of 1/24. The logical value in parentheses is 0 or 1, corresponding to 0 or 24 hours. The result returned by the formula is automatically issued in time format due to the sequence of the operands.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multitables.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Applying Multiple Sheets				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multitables.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154759				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>sheets; inserting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting; sheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sheets; selecting multiple\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sheets; simultaneous multiple filling\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>appending sheets\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multitables.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154759				0	bs	\<variable id=\"multitables\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/multitables.xhp\" name=\"Applying Multiple Sheets\"\>Applying Multiple Sheets\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multitables.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148576				0	bs	Inserting a Sheet				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multitables.xhp	0	help	par_id3154731				0	bs	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Insert - Sheet\</item\> to insert a new sheet or an existing sheet from another file.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multitables.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154491				0	bs	Selecting Multiple Sheets				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multitables.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155600				0	bs	Undoing a Selection				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multitables.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156382				0	bs	Calculating Across Multiple Sheets				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\multitables.xhp	0	help	par_id3155333				0	bs	You can refer to a range of sheets in a formula by specifying the first and last sheet of the range, for example, \<item type=\"literal\"\>=SUM(Sheet1.A1:Sheet3.A1) \</item\>sums up all A1 cells on Sheet1 through Sheet3.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Copying Formulas				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151113				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>formulas; copying\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>copying; formulas\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151113				0	bs	\<variable id=\"formula_copy\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/formula_copy.xhp\" name=\"Copying Formulas\"\>Copying Formulas\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3156424				0	bs	There are various ways to copy a formula. One suggested method is:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3150439				0	bs	Select the cell containing the formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	bs	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Copy\</emph\>, or press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\> \<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\> \<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\> \</switchinline\>+ C to copy it.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3159155				0	bs	Select the cell into which you want the formula to be copied.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3153728				0	bs	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Paste\</emph\>, or press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\> \<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\> \<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\> \</switchinline\>+V. The formula will be positioned in the new cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3149961				0	bs	If you want to copy a formula into multiple cells, there is a quick and easy way to copy into adjacent cell areas:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3149400				0	bs	Select the cell containing the formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3154018				0	bs	Position the mouse on the bottom right of the highlighted border of the cell, and continue holding down the mouse button until the pointer changes to a cross-hair symbol.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749				0	bs	With the mouse button pressed, drag it down or to the right over all the cells into which you want to copy the formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3153714				0	bs	When you release the mouse button, the formula will be copied into the cells and automatically adjusted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\formula_copy.xhp	0	help	par_id3156385				0	bs	If you do not want values and texts to be automatically adjusted, then hold down the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\> \<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\> \<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\> \</switchinline\> key when dragging. Formulas, however, are always adjusted accordingly.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\super_subscript.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Text Superscript / Subscript				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\super_subscript.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151112				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>superscript text in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>subscript text in cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; text super/sub\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>characters;superscript/subscript\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\super_subscript.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151112				0	bs	\<variable id=\"super_subscript\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/super_subscript.xhp\" name=\"Text Superscript / Subscript\"\>Text Superscript / Subscript\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\super_subscript.xhp	0	help	par_id3154684				0	bs	In the cell, select the character that you want to put in superscript or subscript.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\super_subscript.xhp	0	help	par_id3150439				0	bs	If, for example, you want to write H20 with a subscript 2, select the 2 in the cell (not in the input line).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\super_subscript.xhp	0	help	par_id3149260				0	bs	Open the context menu for the selected character and choose \<emph\>Character\</emph\>. You will see the \<emph\>Character\</emph\> dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\super_subscript.xhp	0	help	par_id3153142				0	bs	Click the \<emph\>Font Position\</emph\> tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\super_subscript.xhp	0	help	par_id3153954				0	bs	Select the \<emph\>Subscript\</emph\> option and click OK.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\super_subscript.xhp	0	help	par_id3153876				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/05020500.xhp\" name=\"Context menu - Character - Font Position\"\>Context menu - Character - Font Position\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Assigning Formats by Formula				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145673				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>formats; assigning by formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell formats; assigning by formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>STYLE function example\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell styles;assigning by formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas;assigning cell formats\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145673	13			0	bs	\<variable id=\"cellstyle_by_formula\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cellstyle_by_formula.xhp\" name=\"Assigning Formats by Formula\"\>Assigning Formats by Formula\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150275				0	bs	The STYLE() function can be added to an existing formula in a cell. For example, together with the CURRENT function, you can color a cell depending on its value. The formula =...+STYLE(IF(CURRENT()>3, "Red", "Green")) applies the cell style "Red" to cells if the value is greater than 3, otherwise the cell style "Green" is applied.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3151385				0	bs	If you would like to apply a formula to all cells in a selected area, you can use the\<emph\> Find & Replace\</emph\> dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149456				0	bs	Select all the desired cells.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3148797				0	bs	Select the menu command \<emph\>Edit - Find & Replace\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767	18			0	bs	For the \<emph\>Search for\</emph\> term, enter: .*				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153770				0	bs	".*" is a regular expression that designates the contents of the current cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3153143	20			0	bs	Enter the following formula in the \<emph\>Replace with\</emph\> field: =&+STYLE(IF(CURRENT()>3;"Red";"Green"))				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975				0	bs	The "&" symbol designates the current contents of the \<emph\>Search for\</emph\> field. The line must begin with an equal sign, since it is a formula. It is assumed that the cell styles "Red" and "Green" already exist.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3149262				0	bs	Mark the fields \<link href=\"text/shared/01/02100000.xhp\" name=\"Regular expressions\"\>\<emph\>Regular expressions\</emph\>\</link\> and \<emph\>Current selection only\</emph\>. Click \<emph\>Find All\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3144767				0	bs	All cells with contents that were included in the selection are now highlighted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cellstyle_by_formula.xhp	0	help	par_id3147127				0	bs	Click\<emph\> Replace all\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Naming Cells				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147434				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>cells; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>names; for cells\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>values; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>constants definition\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell ranges; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defining names; cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas; defining names\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell names;in formulas\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147434				0	bs	\<variable id=\"value_with_name\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/value_with_name.xhp\" name=\"Naming Cells\"\>Naming Cells\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3153954				0	bs	Select a cell or range of cells, then choose \<emph\>Insert - Names - Define\</emph\>. The \<emph\>Define Names\</emph\> dialog appears.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3156283				0	bs	Type the name of the selected area in the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> field. Click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>. The newly defined name appears in the list below. Click OK to close the dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3154942				0	bs	If you type the name in a formula, after the first few characters entered you will see the entire name as a tip.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3154510				0	bs	Press the Enter key in order to accept the name from the tip.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3150749				0	bs	If more than one name starts with the same characters, you can scroll through all the names using the Tab key.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\value_with_name.xhp	0	help	par_id3153711				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04070100.xhp\" name=\"Insert - Names - Define\"\>Insert - Names - Define\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Protecting Cells from Changes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	bm_id3146119				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>protecting;cells and sheets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; protecting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cell protection; enabling\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>sheets; protecting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>documents; protecting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cells; hiding for printing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>modifying; sheet protection\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>changing; document protection\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>hiding;formulas\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulas;hiding\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146119				0	bs	\<variable id=\"cell_protect\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/guide/cell_protect.xhp\" name=\"Protecting Cells from Changes\"\>Protecting Cells from Changes\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066B				0	bs	Select the cells that you want to specify the cell protection options for.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3149019				0	bs	Choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Format - Cells\</item\> and click the \<emph\>Cell Protection\</emph\> tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3152898	10			0	bs	Select \<emph\>Protected\</emph\> to prevent changes to the contents and the format of a cell. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN1069A				0	bs	Select \<emph\>Hide formula\</emph\> to hide and to protect formulas from changes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106A1				0	bs	Select \<emph\>Hide when printing\</emph\> to hide protected cells in the printed document. The cells are not hidden onscreen.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3152872				0	bs	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3145362				0	bs	Apply the protection options.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106C0				0	bs	To protect the cells from being changed / viewed / printed according to your settings in the \<emph\>Format - Cells\</emph\> dialog, choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Tools - Protect Document - Sheet\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106CF				0	bs	(Optional) Enter a password.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_idN106D2				0	bs	If you forget your password, you cannot deactivate the protection. If you only want to protect cells from accidental changes, set the sheet protection, but do not enter a password.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\guide\cell_protect.xhp	0	help	par_id3153810	13			0	bs	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
