helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152596				0	bs	\<variable id=\"brackets\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/brackets.xhp\" name=\"Merging Formula Parts in Brackets\"\>Merging Formula Parts in Brackets\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154511				0	bs	Inserting fractions into formulas				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3146971				0	bs	In the case of a fraction whose numerator and denominator consist of a product, a sum, and so on, the values that belong together must be bracketed together.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3149021				0	bs	Use the following syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3083280				0	bs	{a + c} over 2 = m				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3154703				0	bs	or				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\brackets.xhp	0	help	par_id3150297				0	bs	m = {a + c} over 2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Changing Default Attributes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145792				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>attributes; changing in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>font attributes;changing defaults\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting;changing default attributes\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defaults;changing default formatting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>changing;default formatting\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145792				0	bs	\<variable id=\"attributes\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/attributes.xhp\" name=\"Changing Default Attributes\"\>Changing Default Attributes\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154484				0	bs	Can default formats in $[officename] Math be modified?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3148870				0	bs	Some parts of formulas are always formatted bold or italic by default.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3150210				0	bs	You can remove these attributes using "nbold" and "nitalic". Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3143231				0	bs	a + b				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3153810				0	bs	nitalic a + bold b.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3149872				0	bs	In the second formula, the a is not italic. The b is bold. You cannot change the plus sign by this method.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Inserting Brackets				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153415				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>brackets; inserting in %PRODUCTNAME Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;brackets\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>distances between brackets\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153415				0	bs	\<variable id=\"parentheses\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/parentheses.xhp\" name=\"Inserting Brackets\"\>Inserting Brackets\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150751				0	bs	In \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> Math, can brackets be shown separately so that the distance between them is freely definable?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3083281				0	bs	You can set individual brackets using "left" and "right", but the distance between the brackets will not be fixed, as they adapt to the argument. Nevertheless, there is a way to display brackets so that the distance between them is fixed. To accomplish this, place a "\\" (backslash) before the normal brackets. These brackets now behave like any other symbol and the alignment is the same as with other symbols:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3155960				0	bs	left lbrace x right none				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3148489				0	bs	size *2 langle x rangle				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\parentheses.xhp	0	help	par_id3150344				0	bs	size *2 \\langle x \\rangle				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149018				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>accessibility; $[officename] Math shortcuts\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149018				0	bs	\<variable id=\"keyboard\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/keyboard.xhp\" name=\"Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)\"\>Shortcuts ($[officename] Math Accessibility)\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150298				0	bs	You can control $[officename] Math without a mouse.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150343				0	bs	Inserting a Formula Directly				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3154659				0	bs	If you want to insert a formula into a text document, and you already know the correct writing, you can proceed as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153818				0	bs	Write the formula into your text				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153915				0	bs	Select the formula				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150213				0	bs	Choose the command \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154767				0	bs	Inserting a Formula using a Window				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3149875				0	bs	If you want to use the $[officename] Math interface to edit a formula, choose the command \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\> without any text selected.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391				0	bs	The cursor waits in the Commands window and you can type the formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\newline.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Entering Line Breaks				2002-02-02 02:02:02
