helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Debugging a Basic Program				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153344				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>debugging Basic programs\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Basic; debugging\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables; observing values\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>watching variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>run-time errors in Basic\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>error codes in Basic\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>breakpoints\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Call Stack window\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153344				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01030300.xhp\"\>Debugging a Basic Program\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159224				0	bs	Breakpoints and Single Step Execution				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150682				0	bs	You can check each line in your Basic program for errors using single step execution. Errors are easily traced since you can immediately see the result of each step. A pointer in the breakpoint column of the Editor indicates the current line. You can also set a breakpoint if you want to force the program to be interrupted at a specific position.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147303				0	bs	Double-click in the \<emph\>breakpoint\</emph\> column at the left of the Editor window to toggle a breakpoint at the corresponding line. When the program reaches a breakpoint, the program execution is interrupted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155805	8			0	bs	The \<emph\>single step \</emph\>execution using the \<emph\>Single Step\</emph\> icon causes the program to branch into procedures and functions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151110				0	bs	The procedure step execution using the \<emph\>Procedure Step\</emph\> icon causes the program to skip over procedures and functions as a single step.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153825				0	bs	Properties of a Breakpoint				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147574				0	bs	The properties of a breakpoint are available through its context menu by right-clicking the breakpoint in the breakpoint column.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3148473	10			0	bs	You can \<emph\>activate\</emph\> and \<emph\>deactivate\</emph\> a breakpoint by selecting \<emph\>Active\</emph\> from its context menu. When a breakpoint is deactivated, it does not interrupt the program execution. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3159413				0	bs	Select \<emph\>Properties\</emph\> from the context menu of a breakpoint or select \<emph\>Breakpoints\</emph\> from the context menu of the breakpoint column to call the \<emph\>Breakpoints\</emph\> dialog where you can specify other breakpoint options.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3156280				0	bs	The list displays all \<emph\>breakpoints\</emph\> with the corresponding line number in the source code. You can activate or deactivate a selected breakpoint by checking or clearing the \<emph\>Active\</emph\> box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3158407				0	bs	The \<emph\>Pass Count\</emph\> specifies the number of times the breakpoint can be passed over before the program is interrupted. If you enter 0 (default setting) the program is always interrupted as soon as a breakpoint is encountered.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153968				0	bs	Click \<emph\>Delete\</emph\> to remove the breakpoint from the program.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150439				0	bs	Observing the Value of Variables				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368				0	bs	You can monitor the values of a variable by adding it to the \<emph\>Watch\</emph\> window. To add a variable to the list of watched variables, type the variable name in the \<emph\>Watch\</emph\> text box and press Enter.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146986				0	bs	The values of variables are only displayed if they are in scope. Variables that are not defined at the current source code location display ("Out of Scope") instead of a value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145272				0	bs	You can also include arrays in the Watch window. If you enter the name of an array variable without an index value in the Watch text box, the content of the entire array is displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145749				0	bs	If you rest the mouse over a predefined variable in the Editor at run-time, the content of the variable is displayed in a pop-up box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148618				0	bs	The Call Stack Window				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154491				0	bs	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_BASICIDE_STACKWINDOW_LIST\"\>Provides an overview of the call hierarchy of procedures and functions.\</ahelp\> You can determine which procedures and functions called which other procedures and functions at the current point in the source code.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01030300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150594				0	bs	List of Run-Time Errors				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	CreateUnoListener Function [Runtime]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155150				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>CreateUnoListener function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155150				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03132000.xhp\" name=\"CreateUnoListener Function [Runtime]\"\>CreateUnoListener Function [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149346				0	bs	Creates a Listener instance.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153681				0	bs	Many Uno interfaces let you register listeners on a special listener interface. This allows you to listen for specific events and call up the appropriate listener method. The CreateUnoListener function waits for the called listener interface and then passes the interface an object that the interface supports. This object is then passed to the method to register the listener.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148685				0	bs	Syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3143228				0	bs	oListener = CreateUnoListener( Prefixname, ListenerInterfaceName )				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147574				0	bs	Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154046				0	bs	The following example is based on a Basic library object.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3155136				0	bs	Dim oListener				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3148944				0	bs	oListener = CreateUnoListener( "ContListener_","com.sun.star.container.XContainerListener" )				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149294				0	bs	The CreateUnoListener method requires two parameters. The first is a prefix and is explained in detail below. The second parameter is the fully qualified name of the Listener interface that you want to use.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149670				0	bs	The Listener must then be added to the Broadcaster Object. This is done by calling the appropriate method for adding a Listener. These methods always follow the pattern "addFooListener", where "Foo" is the Listener Interface Type, without the 'X'. In this example, the addContainerListener method is called to register the XContainerListener:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154164				0	bs	Dim oLib				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154940				0	bs	oLib = BasicLibraries.Library1 ' Library1 must exist				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150359				0	bs	oLib.addContainerListener( oListener ) ' Register the listener				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154138				0	bs	The Listener is now registered. When an event occurs, the corresponding Listener calls the appropriate method from the com.sun.star.container.XContainerListener Interface.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3148922				0	bs	The prefix calls registered Listeners from Basic-subroutines. The Basic run-time system searches for Basic-subroutines or functions that have the name "PrefixListenerMethode" and calls them when found. Otherwise, a run-time error occurs.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150768				0	bs	In this example, the Listener-Interface uses the following methods:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3151176				0	bs	disposing:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145173				0	bs	Listener base interface (com.sun.star.lang.XEventListener): base interface for all Listener Interfaces				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3156212				0	bs	elementInserted:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3159254				0	bs	Method of the com.sun.star.container.XContainerListener interface				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3147287				0	bs	elementRemoved:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3146119				0	bs	Method of the com.sun.star.container.XContainerListener interface				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153951				0	bs	elementReplaced:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154013				0	bs	Method of the com.sun.star.container.XContainerListener interface				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3147435				0	bs	In this example, the prefix is ContListener_. The following subroutines must therefore be implemented in Basic:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3155411				0	bs	ContListener_disposing				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3146923				0	bs	ContListener_elementInserted				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3147318				0	bs	ContListener_elementRemoved				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3152578				0	bs	ContListener_elementReplaced				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150592				0	bs	An event structure type that contains information about an event exists for every Listener type. When a Listener method is called, an instance of this event is passed to the method as a parameter. Basic Listener methods can also call these event objects, so long as the appropriate parameter is passed in the Sub declaration. For example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3149582				0	bs	Sub ContListener_disposing( oEvent )				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03132000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153876				0	bs	MsgBox "disposing"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
