helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	Using Procedures and Functions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149456				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>procedures\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;using\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;passing to procedures and functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>parameters;for procedures and functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>parameters;passing by reference or value\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;scope\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>scope of variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>GLOBAL variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PUBLIC variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>PRIVATE variables\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;return value type\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>return value type of functions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149456				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020300.xhp\"\>Using Procedures and Functions\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150767				0	bs	The following describes the basic use of procedures and functions in $[officename] Basic.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151215				0	bs	When you create a new module, $[officename] Basic automatically inserts a SUB called "Main". This default name has nothing to do with the order or the starting point of a $[officename] Basic project. You can also safely rename this SUB.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154124				0	bs	Procedures (SUBS) and functions (FUNCTIONS) help you maintaining a structured overview by separating a program into logical pieces.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193				0	bs	One benefit of procedures and functions is that, once you have developed a program code containing task components, you can use this code in another project.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153770				0	bs	Passing Variables to Procedures (SUB) and Functions (FUNCTION)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155414				0	bs	Variables can be passed to both procedures and functions. The SUB or FUNCTION must be declared to expect parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3163710				0	bs	SUB SubName(\<emph\>Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...\</emph\>)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3151114				0	bs	Program code				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975				0	bs	END SUB				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577				0	bs	The SUB is called using the following syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3159154				0	bs	SubName(Value1, Value2,...)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147124				0	bs	The parameters passed to a SUB must fit to those specified in the SUB declaration.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3147397				0	bs	The same process applies to FUNCTIONS. In addition, functions always return a function result. The result of a function is defined by assigning the return value to the function name:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149412				0	bs	FUNCTION FunctionName(Parameter1 As Type, Parameter2 As Type,...) As Type				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3156284				0	bs	Program code				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145799				0	bs	\<emph\>FunctionName=Result\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150716				0	bs	End Function				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153839				0	bs	The FUNCTION is called using the following syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3146914				0	bs	Variable=FunctionName(Parameter1, Parameter2,...)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_idN107B3				0	bs	You can also use the fully qualified name to call a procedure or function:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Library.Module.Macro()\</item\>\<br/\> For example, to call the Autotext macro from the Gimmicks library, use the following command:\<br/\>\<item type=\"literal\"\>Gimmicks.AutoText.Main()\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156276				0	bs	Passing Variables by Value or Reference				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3155765				0	bs	Parameters can be passed to a SUB or a FUNCTION either by reference or by value. Unless otherwise specified, a parameter is always passed by reference. That means that a SUB or a FUNCTION gets the parameter and can read and modify its value.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145640				0	bs	If you want to pass a parameter by value insert the key word "ByVal" in front of the parameter when you call a SUB or FUNCTION, for example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150042				0	bs	Result = Function(\<emph\>ByVal\</emph\> Parameter)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149258				0	bs	In this case, the original content of the parameter will not be modified by the FUNCTION since it only gets the value and not the parameter itself.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150982				0	bs	Scope of Variables				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149814				0	bs	A variable defined within a SUB or FUNCTION, only remains valid until the procedure is exited. This is known as a "local" variable. In many cases, you need a variable to be valid in all procedures, in every module of all libraries, or after a SUB or FUNCTION is exited. This property is controlled in a variable declaration through key words in the \<emph\>Dim\</emph\> statement.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154186				0	bs	Declaring Variables Outside a SUB or FUNCTION				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150208				0	bs	DIM GLOBAL VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3145258				0	bs	The variable is valid as long as the $[officename] session lasts.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3153198				0	bs	DIM PUBLIC VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150088				0	bs	The variable is valid in all modules.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3158212				0	bs	DIM PRIVATE VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152994				0	bs	The variable is only valid in this module.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150886				0	bs	DIM VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3150368				0	bs	The variable is only valid in this module.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154368				0	bs	Saving Variable Content after Exiting a SUB or FUNCTION				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3156288				0	bs	DIM STATIC VarName As TYPENAME				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3154486				0	bs	The variable retains its value until the next time the FUNCTION or SUB is entered. The declaration must exist inside a SUB or a FUNCTION.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155809				0	bs	Specifying the Return Value Type of a FUNCTION				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3149404				0	bs	As with variables, include a type-declaration character after the function name, or the type indicated by "As" and the corresponding key word at the end of the parameter list to define the type of the function's return value, for example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020300.xhp	0	help	par_id3152899				0	bs	Function WordCount(WordText as String) \<emph\>as Integer\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090302.xhp	0	help	tit				0	bs	GoTo Statement [Runtime]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090302.xhp	0	help	bm_id3159413				0	bs	\<bookmark_value\>GoTo statement\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090302.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159413				0	bs	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03090302.xhp\" name=\"GoTo Statement [Runtime]\"\>GoTo Statement [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03090302.xhp	0	help	par_id3153379				0	bs	Continues program execution within a Sub or Function at the procedure line indicated by a label.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
